I've enjoyed reading this thread started by Amara, & I'd like to add
some personal comments & possibly helpful information. Amara
commented:

    First and foremost, I see a large lack of awareness and interest
    about topics that many women are concerned with today.  This isn't
    a problem, except that you might be missing an opportunity to
    think and talk about how extropian ideas might be relevant to
    helping women as well as men.

         * A recent example:  Most women's difficulty to both succeed
         in a career and raise a child.  I can't think of a single
         issue more important to a woman in her 20's and 30's who is
         very actively persuing her interests and finding she's
         successful. ...

I have two comments. First the helpful(?) information.

I think the science of embryo freezing is more developed than egg
freezing.  So young women wanting to delay child-bearing might be
well-advised to get their extracted egg(s) fertilized -in vitro- by
donated sperm and freezing the resulting embryo(s). If such a young
woman wants to improve the genetic odds for a good result, she might
enquire if the late Dr. Shockley's "genius only" sperm bank is still
in business.  Even if not, most donated sperm is from healthy young
medical students, above the average population, anyway.

My second comment is to question the -desire- to reproduce, especially
among Extropians, who hope, through cryonics, to acheive something
approaching physical immortality for themselves.  Having children has
always been a poor substitute for immortality, but until the last few
decades it was all that was available.

I can understand the human desire to "go through the motions" of
attempting to have child(ren). But the thought of actually being a
parent, especially in this society in which the state intervenes with
a heavy hand in parent-child relationships, I would regard with the
same anticipation as an 18-year prison sentence at hard labor.

I formed this opinion while in high school, and followed up on it in
my early twenties with a vasectomy (male sterilization surgery). It
was unusual at that time (early 60's) for young, single men to request
this operation, and I had a hell of a time finding a urologist who
would agree to perform the surgery.

So perhaps the women (and men?) here who are concerned with the
career/child-bearing conflict would enlighten us (me) with some
introspection about this desire or need to procreate, which I
find absent in myself.

         How about discussions about the studies of MALE
         contraceptives?  That handy little extropian device called
         the condom might prove to be an interesting topic (what is
         your favorite condom?).  ...

In my experience, the best male contraceptive is the vasectomy.
Unfortunately, these days, it does not prevent transmission of
disease, so I'm glad that I'm married & monogamous.

         *Interpersonal and relationship issues.  Why hasn't the
         "sacred" topic of marriage ever come up on this list?  Why
         hasn't interpersonnal contracts like cohabitation contracts
         ever been discussed?

         Suggestions:  Given the horrendous baggage with church and
         state (and IRS), I would like to see some discussions on why
         some extropians got married.

Up until a year or so after I met my first wife, I was convinced that
I would never marry. I hoped to find someone who would live with me
without "benefit" of marriage.  Eventually I did, and we were together
about a year. In retrospect, I was not then at a point where I was
willing to "settle down."

I had many discussions about marriage with my first wife-to-be. Her
view, which eventually became my view, was that marriage is desireable
as an impediment to breaking up over trivialities. Going through the
divorce process forces the parties to take time to rethink the
decision to split up. Marriage gives both parties some measure of
security that the relationship is going to last.

But what finally got me to the altar the first time was my first
wife's immigration status. She would be deported to Guatemala unless
she could marry an American citizen.  I thought it appropriate to use
the tools of the state (marriage) against the state (INS).

My first marriage lasted three years. I expect the critical factor in
our splitup was her children from previous marriages, who were living
with her. Because I didn't want any children living with me, we
maintained separate residences throughout our marriage, although we
otherwise had a "normal" marriage. I thought at the beginning of our
marriage that I would have an opportunity to "play father" without any
real (especially financial) responsibilities, but that experiment was
a failure.

By the end of my first marriage, I was convinced of the basic
desirability of marriage, and immediately set out to find another
wife. I was fortunate to meet my second wife even before my first
divorce was final.  We are still married after six years. This is her
first marriage and she is childless and agreed to a marraige sans
children.

         How does one in daily life handle community property and
         separate property in such a union?

In both my marriages, we had a pre-marital agreement which kept our
separate property separate (this is essential in California). Since we
maintained separate households in my first marriage, keeping finances
separate was not a problem.

In my second marriage, I pay for common expenses of food & shelter.
She does most of the cooking & household chores.  My wife works & pays
for her own car, clothing, jewelry (except for gifts).  She is also
responsible for her own financial security after I am in a
cryo-capsule.

         What methods did you use to avoid the religious aspect (or
         did you?)?

My first marriage ceremony was performed by a "minister" of the
Universal Life Church.  The second was performed by a "minister" of
the "Universal Libertarian Fellowship" (now defunct I hear).

         Isn't it bothersome that when you get a credit check, your
         mate's credit is automatically checked too?  What kind of
         cohabitation contracts (if any) have people here invoked?
