    Second, A lack of humor on this list.  ...  This list is probably
    just a bit too dry for many women's tastes.  (I'll have to post a
    wonderful somewhat dirty joke that I just heard yesterday.)

But you never -did-, Amara. Please do!

I tend to agree with most of Tim May's long essay on "evolutionary"
differences between male and female.  I think someone later noted that
these male and female roles -are- different from ours in some other
cultures.

My contribution to the Genetics/Culture or Nature/Nurture debate is to
note that over a period of many generations, these factors can
reinforce -each other-.  A genetic divergence in a more-or-less
isolated population will affect the culture. e.g. the pygmy culture
prefers shorter partners; the Watusi culture taller. A cultural
divergence becomes reinforced genetically by influencing choice of
mates. i.e. who is allowed to reproduce.

So "cultural" differences between the sexes which have persisted for
many generations are now also, to some degree, in our genes as well,
and such genetic influences will resist attempts to change the
"culture" for a long time.

