Subject: Humorous Thought: HiTech HiJack

Re: Mr. Cromwell's suggestion about hijacking the Hubble Telescope.

The HST communicates with the ground through the Tracking and
Data Relay satellites that NASA owns.  There are two active
satellites, and they are controlled by a ground station at White
Sands, New Mexico.  The antennas on the Hubble point at the GEO
TDRS satellites, so transmitting directly to Hubble won't do
much good.  You could place a transmitter with a stronger signal
(bigger dish, higher wattage, or come combination) near White
Sands and swamp the signal, but a hundred foot dish in the New
Mexico desert would stick out like a sore thumb, and you would be
run down pretty quickly.  The data goes from White Sands to the
Space Telescope Science Institute via a domestic comsat, so that
would be another point of attack.

A friend of mine who worked at Bell Labs in New Jersey always wanted
to hijack the Muzak channel (which goes via satellite).  The radio
telescope used by Penzis and Wilson to discover the 3K microwave
background is located at that site (i.e. there was a big, unused
antenna lying around), and Bell labs has lots of comm gear available.
He wanted to substitute stuff like the 1812 Overture and the Ride
of the Valkyries for the ususal Muzak, and wake some people up.
He figured he had about 15-20 minutes to get on and off before the
FCC started calling around to all the big antenna owners and asking
if their dish was pointed at the satellite the Muzak signal was on.

I would suggest blackmail rather than hijacking if you
want to bend NASA towards new launch technologies.  Or simple fraud
(bid low to get a contract, run up charges (assuming it is cost-plus)
and actually just run a shell, with most of the money funneled 
overseas.  A combination might even be better (blackmail the
program manager to give you the contract and ensure he won't investigate
you too quick).  Of course, I dont recommend these courses unless
all others have failed, as blackmail doesn't always work.

