Date: Fri, 10 Dec 93 10:27:26 CST
From: eder@hsvaic.hv.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: Humorous Thought: HiTech HiJack

Re: Mr. Israel's comments on Hubble/Spysat similarity.

Like most legends, the claim that Hubble and spy satellites are
very similar (ie the difference is in which way they point), has
a grain of truth.  In this case, a fairly large grain.

My source of information is a Boeing guy I worked with about
10 years ago.  Boeing built the Graphite-Epoxy structure that
holds the mirrors and instruments in place relative to each other
(a finicky job in itself, almost as bad as making the optics
themselves).  We worked for Perkin Elmer (now GM-Hughes Danbury
Optical division), who had the overall responsibility for the
optics.  Lockheed had the overall responsibility for the
'spacecraft', which means the power, pointing, etc.  The Boeing
guy went to Lockheed's facilities for some tests.  He noted
that there were a total of 4 bays in the building, each of which
could hold a Hubble sized payload (they were using 1), and the
Lockheed guys were very competant.  His comment to the Lockheed
that 'you guys have done this before, havent you?' was met with
smiles, and silence.  So much for facts.

We can infer that Hubble was built on a heritage of Spy satellites,
but there are a lot of specific differences.  Spy sats want to
fly as low as possible, for better resolution.  Thus they need a
lot of propellant for making up drag, and they want to carry even
more to do orbit adjustments to fool the people on the ground
(they can cover up stuff if the spy sat is on a regular scheduled
orbit), and to fly over crisis locations on shorter notice.

Hubble, on the other hand, has no propulsion at all, because any
fluid sloshing in a tank would totally mess up the pointing, and
the gunk from thrusters would mess up the optics.  Hubble's
optics is used in the UV, which is useless for looking at the Earth,
since the atmosphere won't pass those wavelengths.  Spysat pictures
have to be taken quickly, because you are moving fast, and you
are often imaging in daylight.  Hubble can't look at the Earth or
Moon, they are too bright for the CCDs.  In fact, there is a process
called 'flat fielding' done with CCDs - the cells in the CCD are
slightly variable in electron capacity, being made like any other
small silicon device with some variation in features made by lithography.
To find out the capacity of each cell, you point the telescope
at a bright object and saturate the CCD, then read them out to find out
what the max on each cell is.  Hubble points at clouds -they are
white, and at the speed it is moving they blur past to an even
white and quickly saturate the CCDs.

Dani Eder

